{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_27","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Carson v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2023-12-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"88 F.4th 633; Nos. 22-3386/22-3419\",\"proposition\":\"Counsel may perform deficiently by failing to initiate plea negotiations even when the prosecution made no offer, but Carson failed to show prejudice because nothing established that the government would have negotiated and he had insisted on trial.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—failure to initiate negotiations\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 plea-stage-IAC split queries → Villa citations → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/22-3386/22-3386-2023-12-13.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Kearn\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-01-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"90 F.4th 1301; No. 23-3029\",\"proposition\":\"Affirmed Section 2255 relief and a plea reoffer where counsel gave an inaccurate six-minute explanation that caused rejection of a ten-year offer; the approximately twenty-year sentencing disparity furnished strong objective evidence of prejudice despite the defendant's reluctance to plead.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—advice, rejection, and prejudice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 federal plea ineffective-assistance queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/23-3029/23-3029-2024-01-23.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Sterling\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"99 F.4th 783; No. 23-30069\",\"proposition\":\"As a matter of first impression, held that when reasonable cause exists to question competency, a court may not let the accused represent himself at the competency hearing and must appoint counsel until the doubt is extinguished; no violation occurred because the record supplied no reasonable cause. The court aligned with the Sixth Circuit and distinguished the Second Circuit's more flexible approach.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"attachment and critical stages—competency hearing; waiver\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Sixth Amendment critical-stage circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/23-30069/23-30069-2024-04-25.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Betschart v. Washington County Circuit Court Judges\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-05-31\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2270\",\"proposition\":\"Affirmed a preliminary injunction requiring appointment of counsel within seven days of initial appearance, or release, for jailed indigent defendants. At the preliminary-injunction stage, the absence of counsel likely violated the Sixth Amendment because it prevented preparation for and progression to critical stages; the court also recognized that a bail hearing may itself be critical.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"attachment and timely appointment—pretrial detention\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 attachment appointment-of-counsel federal circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-2270/23-2270-2024-05-31.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Baker v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-2059\",\"proposition\":\"Counsel rendered ineffective assistance by grossly underestimating stacked Section 924(c) exposure before the defendant rejected a plea. A later assertion of innocence is only weakly probative, not dispositive, and a large objective sentencing disparity can establish prejudice; the government was ordered to reoffer the plea.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—sentencing advice and prejudice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Lafler Frye federal circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca3/23-2059/23-2059-2024-07-25.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Osorio-Arellanes\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 20-10003\",\"proposition\":\"A Mexican lawyer advising an indicted defendant during interrogation in Mexico qualified as counsel for Sixth Amendment purposes even though he was not licensed in the United States. Advising a confession to pending charges without a deal, based on a blatant misunderstanding of United States law, was deficient and prejudicial assistance requiring vacatur.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"post-attachment interrogation—identity and effectiveness of counsel\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 post-indictment interrogation counsel circuit queries → published opinion at https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-9th-circuit/116472455.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Farhane v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit, en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-10-31\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"121 F.4th 353; No. 20-1666\",\"proposition\":\"Extended Padilla to hold that counsel must advise a naturalized citizen that pleading guilty creates a risk of denaturalization and consequent deportation; vacated the contrary panel disposition and remanded for application of Strickland's deficiency and prejudice requirements.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—immigration and denaturalization advice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 en banc Sixth Amendment plea queries → published en banc opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/20-1666/20-1666-2024-10-31.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Hohn\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit, en banc\",\"date\":\"2024-12-16\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"123 F.4th 1084; No. 22-3009\",\"proposition\":\"Overruled Shillinger v. Haworth's structural-error and presumed-prejudice rule. Even a purposeful, unjustified government intrusion into confidential attorney-client communications violates the Sixth Amendment only upon a showing of prejudice to the criminal proceeding.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"government intrusion into attorney-client relationship—prejudice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 en banc attorney-client intrusion Sixth Amendment queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/22-3009/22-3009-2024-12-16.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Davis\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eleventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-13\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-12971\",\"proposition\":\"Joined six other circuits in holding that the right to counsel at sentencing entails a corresponding Faretta right of self-representation. A clear and unequivocal request must be honored after a searching inquiry establishes a knowing and intelligent waiver; the sentence was vacated.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"waiver and self-representation—sentencing\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Sixth Amendment waiver sentencing circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca11/22-12971/22-12971-2025-03-13.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Rodriguez-Arvizu\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-393\",\"proposition\":\"A post-indictment Sixth Amendment waiver does not invariably require actual notice of the particular indictment or charges. Under the contextual totality, awareness of counsel rights, potential criminal liability, and the matter's gravity can make a Miranda-based waiver knowing and intelligent; an arrest-warrant notice violation did not justify suppression.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"post-attachment interrogation—knowing waiver\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Massiah waiver indictment-notice queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/23-393/23-393-2025-03-17.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Age\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-30656\",\"proposition\":\"For Massiah purposes, a jail informant becomes a government agent through a combination of a promised, reasonably expected, or received benefit and governmental instruction or control. Prior cooperation and hope of a future benefit were insufficient; after prosecutors instructed the informant not to question the accused, ordinary conversation did not constitute deliberate elicitation.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"Massiah—informant agency and deliberate elicitation\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Massiah jail-informant federal circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/22-30656/22-30656-2025-04-25.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Villa v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-04-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-5973\",\"proposition\":\"Frye's communication duty applies to formal plea offers. A prosecutor's email inviting cooperation but providing no concrete plea terms was merely an invitation to negotiate, so counsel had no Sixth Amendment duty to relay it as an offer.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—formal offer requirement\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Frye formal-offer federal circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/24-5973/24-5973-2025-04-29.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Romeo\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-05-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-6297\",\"proposition\":\"Held that a postjudgment proceeding to forfeit substitute assets is generally not a critical stage carrying a Sixth Amendment right to counsel because it cannot increase the punishment already imposed; the court joined the First Circuit's Saccoccia position.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"critical stages—postjudgment substitute-asset forfeiture\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Sixth Amendment critical-stage forfeiture queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca2/23-6297/23-6297-2025-05-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Pouncy v. Palmer\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-05-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 21-1811\",\"proposition\":\"A defendant's post hoc assertion that he would have accepted a plea must be corroborated by contemporaneous evidence to prove Strickland prejudice. Repeated contemporaneous refusals and assertions of innocence defeated the rejected-offer claim.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—proof of rejected-offer prejudice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 rejected-plea contemporaneous-evidence queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/21-1811/21-1811-2025-05-08.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Brown\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-05-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-7105\",\"proposition\":\"Joined the Fifth Circuit in holding that a rejected-offer ineffective-assistance claimant need not produce contemporaneous evidence that he would have pleaded guilty. Lee's contemporaneous-evidence discussion concerned an accepted plea and does not impose an evidentiary prerequisite in the Lafler posture, sharpening a circuit conflict.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—evidentiary split on prejudice\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 rejected-plea contemporaneous-evidence circuit-split queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-7105/22-7105-2025-05-20.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Riley\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-10-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-1287\",\"proposition\":\"District courts have no freestanding constitutional duty to monitor attorney-client communications about a proposed government disposition or proactively detect and prevent counsel's ineffectiveness, although inquiry may be prudent. The ultimate decision whether to accept or reject a disposition remains the defendant's.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea and disposition decisions—court, counsel, and client roles\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 plea-communication Sixth Amendment circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/24-1287/24-1287-2025-10-09.html\",\"confidence\":\"medium-high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Patel v. United States\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-10-17\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"156 F.4th 342; Nos. 23-2418/23-2795\",\"proposition\":\"Joined other circuits in holding that plea counsel ordinarily must advise only about direct consequences. Padilla's special rule for deportation does not extend to potential False Claims Act liability or collateral-estoppel effects from plea admissions, so failure to warn of those civil consequences was not deficient.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"plea-stage ineffective assistance—direct and collateral consequences\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 Padilla collateral-consequences plea queries → published opinion at https://caselaw.findlaw.com/court/us-3rd-circuit/117733832.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Villarreal v. Texas\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2026-02-25\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. 465; No. 24-557\",\"proposition\":\"A qualified midtestimony recess order that bars consultation only about the defendant's ongoing testimony, while leaving counsel and client free to discuss strategy, pleas, evidence, and other protected subjects, does not violate the Sixth Amendment. The Geders-Perry boundary is substantive and content-based, not controlled simply by the recess's duration.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"critical-stage consultation—limits during testimony recess\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Supreme Court Sixth Amendment right-to-counsel queries → official opinion reproduced at https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-557/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Magallon Guerrero\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-5859\",\"proposition\":\"A warning that the government would provide a lawyer reasonably communicated the appointment right before an indicted defendant confessed. The court left unresolved whether omission of the word 'before' questioning independently rendered the warning defective, but held any error harmless and reiterated that confession-related Sixth Amendment violations ordinarily receive harmless-error rather than structural-error review.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"post-attachment interrogation—warning, waiver, and harmless error\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Massiah waiver warning federal circuit queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca6/24-5859/24-5859-2026-03-03.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Johnsen\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-05-26\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-6689\",\"proposition\":\"Post-charge forensic examination of seized electronic devices is not a critical stage requiring the accused or counsel to be present. The accused is not confronted by the government during laboratory analysis, and adversarial testing at trial can mitigate the risk of prejudice.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"critical stages—digital forensic examination\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 Sixth Amendment critical-stage forensic-analysis queries → published opinion at https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca9/24-6689/24-6689-2026-05-26.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"}}
